##############################################################################
# Entry (MDU draft)
##############################################################################

entry:rosen-1978-formal-system
    a atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:label "Rosen (1978), Fundamentals of Measurement and Representation of Natural Systems, §2.9 Appendix, Definition 2.9.1 (book p. 54)"@en ;

    atlas:statedIn bearer:rosen-1978-fundamentals ;
    atlas:sourceLocation """§2.9 Appendix, DEFINITION 2.9.1, book p. 54 (scan page 72)""" ;

    atlas:verbatim """DEFINITION 2.9.1 A system (or formal system) shall consist of a pair (S, F), where S is a set and F is a family of real-valued mappings defined on S. The elements of F will be called the observables of the formal system.
To each formal system defined as above, we can uniquely associate a set of reduced states S/R_F in the fashion we have described in the previous sections; all of the properties we have developed will be meaningful in the context of these formal systems, and we shall use them without further comment.
Notice that we consider two formal systems (S, F) and (S′, F′) as different if S ≠ S′ and/or F ≠ F′.""" ;
    atlas:displayForm """A system (or formal system) shall consist of a pair (S, F), where S is a set and F is a family of real-valued mappings defined on S. The elements of F will be called the observables of the formal system.""" ;
    atlas:displayContext """To each formal system defined as above, we can uniquely associate a set of reduced states S/R_F in the fashion we have described in the previous sections; all of the properties we have developed will be meaningful in the context of these formal systems, and we shall use them without further comment.""" ;
    atlas:authorCaveat """Throughout this work we will be dealing with three basic undefined terms: system, observable and state. For the present, it will suffice for us to proceed with the usual intuitive meanings of these words: a system is some part of the real world which comprises our object of study; an observable of the system is some characteristic of a system which can, in principle, be measured directly; and a state is a specification of what our system is like at a specific instant of time.""" ;
    atlas:invokesPrimitive prim:set ,
                           prim:element ,
                           prim:pair ,
                           prim:family ,
                           prim:real-valued-mapping ,
                           prim:observable ,
                           prim:reduced-state ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDHC ;
    atlas:encodedBy "claude-opus-5 via atlas/ingest/draft_entry.py; verbatim read against scan page 72 (book p. 54) by Shingai Thornton 2026-09-14" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-09-13"^^xsd:date ;

    rdfs:comment """HUMAN CHECK (2026-09-14, Shingai Thornton, reading the PDF page). Confirmed against the page: the definition sentence as printed, and the identity criterion sentence — "two formal systems (S, F) and (S′, F′) as different if S ≠ S′ and/or F ≠ F′" — including the primes and the ≠ signs. The subscript in the gloss's S/R_F was model-read only (Claude, 200 dpi page image, 2026-09-13) and is not recorded as human-confirmed. Promoted MDU → MDHC on the definition; the five primitives keep their MDU-tagged scope notes until the guided pass; the stance assertion below stays MDU (its verbatim is on book p. xvii, not yet read by a human).

CANDIDATE 1 RULED (2026-09-14, Shingai Thornton): PROPOSITIONS 1 AND 2 ARE A CAVEAT, NOT AN ENTRY. Book p. 26 (scan page 44), page-verified: "PROPOSITION 1 The only meaningful physical events which occur in the world are those represented by the evaluation of observables on states. PROPOSITION 2 Every observable can be regarded as a mapping from states to real numbers." Rosen takes them "as axiomatic in all of what follows". They characterise no system; they fix the relation between two of the three terms he declares undefined on the same page (observable, state). Under the parts-definitions rule Proposition 2 is the defining sentence of prim:real-valued-mapping (harvested 2026-09-13); Proposition 1 is recorded here because it is what makes the formal system's observables the ONLY physical events, the premise any mapping to a things-first tradition (Bunge, Mobus) will have to cross. The undefined-terms passage that precedes them is carried as atlas:authorCaveat: it is the caveat that scopes Definition 2.9.1 to the FORMAL system and leaves the natural system undefined — the hinge on which the 1978/1986 reading turns (see the stance block). Ruled important enough to be read first by anyone citing this entry.

DELIBERATELY NOT ASSERTED
This entry records only that the passage states a definition of "system" at the given locus. No cco is-about relation is asserted between this entry and any subject matter, discipline, or other atlas entry; the catalogue's neutrality invariant forbids inferring topical aboutness from the presence of a definition.

WHAT THE ENCODING LEAVES OUT
The locus is embedded in a longer formal development that the entry does not carry: Definition 2.9.2 (subsystem and extension, S′ ⊆ S and F′ ⊆ F), the remarks that (S, F) is a subsystem of (S, H(S, R)) and that no formal system is a subsystem of (S, ∅), Definition 2.9.3 (morphism as a pair (φ, ψ)), and the closing note that "the class of formal systems becomes a category". Also left out is §2.1's declaration that system, observable and state are three basic undefined terms, together with Propositions 1 and 2, on which Rosen's notion of observable rests; the reduced-state construction S/R_F named in the gloss is developed in earlier sections and is not reproduced here.

CASES
No admits or refuses are recorded: at this locus Rosen offers no example as a system and refuses none. The nearby remark about (S, H(S, R)) and (S, ∅) rules on the subsystem relation, not on systemhood, and so was not treated as a case.

SOURCE FIDELITY
The verbatim follows the human-flagged page-image transcription of book p. 54 (scan page 72), not the tesseract OCR body, which mangles subscripts and set-inclusion signs. The transcription itself is marked "Not yet verified by a human against the page"; the subscripted R_F, the primes on S′ and F′, and the ≠ sign in particular should be checked against the page image at review."""@en .


##############################################################################
# Stance (P3). MDU draft, 2026-09-13. One assertion on the ontic axis; the
# observer axis is a queued ruling, not an assertion (see comment).
##############################################################################

@prefix stance: <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/stance/> .

entry:rosen-1978-formal-system atlas:hasStance stance:rosen-1978-ontic .

stance:rosen-1978-ontic
    a atlas:StanceAssertion ;
    atlas:stanceValue atlas:perspectivist ;
    atlas:verbatim "there exists no universal family of analytic units appropriate for the treatment of all interactions; (2) there are on the contrary many such families of analytic units, all of which are equally “real” and entitled to be treated on the same footing" ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "Introduction, book p. xvii (scan page 16), the four stated conclusions" ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDU ;
    atlas:encodedBy "claude-fable-5-1, drafted 2026-09-13 from the full 1978 read plus Anticipatory Systems §2.1–2.3 (OCR) and the 1986 IJGS paper — UNCHECKED; the verbatim is OCR text not yet read against the page image" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-09-13"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:comment """WHY PERSPECTIVIST AND NOT REALIST OR CONSTRUCTIVIST. Both halves of the scheme's scope note are in the text. Reality is real: §2.1 (p. 26, page-verified) glosses the undefined term system as "some part of the real world which comprises our object of study", and the Introduction (p. xii) says "all observables possess the same degree of physical reality". Access is perspectival and no perspective is privileged: the quoted conclusion, and p. xii, "what you see depends on how you look at things". The 1986 paper (IJGS 13; Facets reprint) adds that systemhood is "an independent category", reducible neither to thinghood nor to set-ness, and rejects reductionism and holism together — a foundations claim, not a retraction of Definition 2.9.1, which scopes itself to the FORMAL system ("A system (or formal system)"; "observables of the formal system") while the natural system stays undefined.

HOW IT DIFFERS FROM BERTALANFFY'S CELL. Bertalanffy locates the perspective in an organism's evolved categories of experience (GST ch. 10: the a priori forms are "organic functions, based upon corporeal and even machine-like structures of the sense organs and the nervous system"), isomorphic to reality within tolerance. Rosen locates it in ANY interaction: a perspective is a meter, and a meter is any system in which the observed system induces a dynamics (§2.2; Introduction p. xii, the second cell "looking at the first through quite different eyes"). Bertalanffy's categories are a special case of Rosen's meters. Same ontic value, wider carrier; recorded here so the census can see the difference rather than the label.

OBSERVER AXIS — QUEUED, NOT ASSERTED. Neither existing value fits. Not constitutive (Klir): describability does not confer systemhood; the natural system is prior and undefined. Not merely selective (Mobus): the meter's interaction does not pick a pre-cut system, it determines which observables and which analytic units are visible ("each mode of interaction, and each set of meters chosen for observing it, determines a set of corresponding 'natural' analytic units", §5.1 p. 95, page-verified). A value such as "interactional" or "revelatory" would be a new concept in ObserverRoleScheme and needs a ruling before minting, per the scheme's rule that values enter only with a user.

Reading is Claude's, from the sources named; Shingai Thornton's stated position (2026-09-13) is that this placement matters for the census because his own scientific stance is Bertalanffian rather than Klirian."""@en .
